Assuming you're unburdened, you get something like this:
(P)ut on (j) circular amulet.
It constricts your throat!
j - an amulet of strangulation (being worn)

So, you get a warning message, and the amulet identifies itself.  If you
decide to ignore this you get:

.(wait)
You find it hard to breathe.
.(wait)
You're gasping for air.
.(wait)
You can no longer breathe.
.(wait)
You're turning blue.
.(wait)
You suffocate.

Now, if you do that when you're overtaxed, then it'll all happen in one
turn.  But hopefully you won't go trying on strange amulets when you're
too overtaxed to move.

Moreover, 91% of the time amulets of change, strangulation, and restful 
sleep are cursed.  So while 42% of random amulets are "nasty" (each of 
those three have a 14% probability), only 6.7% of random uncursed/blessed
amulets are nasty.  So if you do as I suggest, you'll avoid most of the
harmful amulets and get the benefit of the good ones right away. (In fact,
if you name all the cursed ones you find "nasty?", you'll be right 86.8%
of the time).

I also will always identify amulets first when I get an identify scroll.
So the cursed ones and the ones I can't figure out by wearing do get 
identified quickly.

